Found while verifying the renderer (#69) and cross-checked against the construction system. Building is the core verb of a prison-management game and it currently does nothing structural.

## What happens

`ConstructionSystem.finalizeConstruction` is a stub, and says so in its own body:

```ts
private finalizeConstruction(order: BuildOrder): void {
  const { x, y } = order.location;
  const { chunk } = tileToChunk(order.location, this.world.tileChunkSize);
  const chunkState = this.world.getChunk(chunk);
  if (chunkState) {
    // In a full implementation, we'd add entities or mutate cell data.
    // For now, bump the geometry revision to signal a topological change.
    this.world.markGeometryChanged(chunk);
  }
}
```

An order runs its whole lifecycle, consumes materials, reaches `completed` — and the world is unchanged. `x` and `y` are destructured and never used. Only the geometry revision moves, so `TopologyManager` recomputes over geometry that never gained a wall.

## Why it is not a one-line fix

`SparseWorld` has the right API — `setTopEdge` / `setLeftEdge`, so walls live on **tile edges**. But `BuildOrder` carries only:

```ts
readonly definitionId: string;
readonly location: TilePosition;
```

There is no orientation. A wall order cannot express *"the north edge of this tile"* versus *"the west edge"*, so `finalizeConstruction` has nothing to write even if it wanted to. **The data model, not the function body, is what is missing.**

That makes this a change to:

- `BuildOrder` — an edge/orientation field;
- the command that submits an order, and its protocol validation;
- **`ConstructionSnapshot`, and therefore the save schema** — which is why this should be sequenced *after* #70, whose V3 bump is already in flight. Two concurrent changes to that shape would collide;
- the renderer, which already draws both edge geometry and in-progress orders, so it should need little — see `docs/RENDERING.md`.

## Two related defects in the same area

**Nothing in the UI submits a build order.** Grepping `src/ui/` and `src/main.ts` for the construction command returns nothing, so building is unreachable from the running app regardless of the above. The HUD's Build tab now exists with nothing behind it.

**`BUILDABLE_REGISTRY` is a two-entry `Map` with hard-coded English names** — `wall-brick` → "Brick Wall", `door-wooden` → "Wooden Door". Every other catalog in `src/content/` carries a `nameKey` and goes through the localization contract (ADR 0011); this one bypasses it and lives in `src/simulation/`, where translated text is explicitly not allowed. It also has no relationship to the 18-room and object catalogs the rest of the game uses. `docs/HUD_PROJECTIONS.md` records this as the reason no construction projection was written.

## Scope

- [ ] `BuildOrder` can express which edge a wall occupies, and `finalizeConstruction` writes it.
- [ ] Completing an order produces geometry `TopologyManager` and `RoomSystem` actually see — proven by a room becoming enclosed *because* walls were built, not because geometry was set directly in a test.
- [ ] Buildables move to the content-catalog convention with message keys, or the reason they cannot is recorded.
- [ ] The schema change goes through the version + migration contract, **after #70**.
- [ ] A build command is reachable from the UI (may split into its own issue).

## Nothing regressed

This has never worked; it is a gap, not a break. But it is the gap between "a simulation with a construction system" and "a game you can build a prison in".

Related: #70 (persistence, owns the schema bump), #69 (renderer, draws the geometry this would produce), `docs/HUD_PROJECTIONS.md`.
